/* Original mathematics lessons: definitions, hand calculations and executable checks. */
function mathModule(track,id,title,subtitle){const m=module(track+'-'+id,title,subtitle,track==='la'?'Linear algebra':'Calculus & optimization');m.track=track;return m;}
function ml(m,x){const number=m.lessons.length+1,shift=(number+COURSE.indexOf(m))%x.check.options.length,opts=x.check.options.slice();for(let i=0;i<shift;i++)opts.push(opts.shift());const check={...x.check,options:opts,answer:(x.check.answer-shift+opts.length)%opts.length};lesson(m,String(number).padStart(2,'0')+' · '+x.title,x.p,x.code,'Output is in the worked solution below.',x.steps.join('\n'),x.trap,x.task,x.solution,check);const l=m.lessons.at(-1);Object.assign(l,{formula:x.formula,mathSteps:x.steps,application:x.application,mathAnswer:x.answer,visual:x.visual,recap:x.recap||null});l.check.id=l.id+'-check';}
const np=`import numpy as np\nnp.set_printoptions(precision=4, suppress=True)\n`;
let lm=mathModule('la','vectors','Vectors & geometric intuition','Coordinates, linear combinations, lengths, angles and linear maps');
ml(lm,{title:'Scalars, vectors and linear combinations',p:[
'A scalar is one number; a vector is an object that can be added and scaled. In R² or R³, draw a vector as an arrow from the origin. Its coordinates describe it relative to an ordered basis. A point and a displacement can have the same coordinate list, but their roles differ.',
'Add vectors component by component and multiply each component by a scalar. The linear combination a u + b v scales two arrows and adds them head-to-tail. Negative coefficients reverse a direction. A zero coefficient omits a vector; the zero vector is always a linear combination.',
'The span of a set is every vector obtainable by its linear combinations. One nonzero vector spans a line through the origin. Two nonparallel vectors in R² span the entire plane. Two parallel vectors still span only a line, no matter how many times they are repeated.',
'Translate a geometric question into equations in the coefficients. To determine whether w is in span(u,v), solve a u+b v=w. This is the first bridge between geometry and a system of linear equations. You do not need calculus for any of these operations.'
],formula:'a u + b v = (a u₁+b v₁, …, a uₙ+b vₙ)ᵀ',steps:['Let u=(1,2), v=(2,−1). Find 3u+2v.','3u=(3,6) and 2v=(4,−2).','Add components: 3u+2v=(7,4).','Conversely, solve a+2b=7 and 2a−b=4 to recover a=3,b=2.'],application:'Feature vectors, forces and image pixels all use weighted combinations. A linear prediction is a weighted combination of input features.',trap:'A line not passing through the origin is an affine set, not a vector subspace.',task:'For u=(1,2),v=(2,−1), compute −u+3v and explain the reversed arrow.',answer:'−u+3v=(5,−5). The coefficient −1 reverses u.',code:np+`u=np.array([1,2]);v=np.array([2,-1])
print(3*u+2*v)`,solution:np+`u=np.array([1,2]);v=np.array([2,-1])
print(-u+3*v)`,check:q('The span of one nonzero vector in R³ is…',['A line through the origin','All of R³','A plane not through the origin','Only the vector itself'],0,'All scalar multiples form a line; zero and negative multiples are included.'),visual:'vectors'});
ml(lm,{title:'Dot products, norms and angles',p:[
'For real coordinate vectors, uᵀv is the sum of products of corresponding components. The Euclidean norm is the square root of uᵀu. It measures length and is zero only for the zero vector. The 1-norm sums absolute values; the infinity norm is the largest absolute component.',
'The dot product equals ||u|| ||v|| cos θ for nonzero vectors. Positive means an acute angle, negative an obtuse angle, and zero means orthogonality. The angle of the zero vector is undefined even though its dot product with every vector is zero.',
'Cauchy–Schwarz bounds |uᵀv| by ||u|| ||v||. Equality holds when the vectors are linearly dependent, including the zero cases. The triangle inequality says ||u+v||≤||u||+||v||. These inequalities are useful for checking whether a proposed numeric answer is even possible.',
'Normalize a nonzero vector by dividing it by its norm. Normalization changes length to one without changing direction. It does not make two arbitrary vectors orthogonal. In complex vector spaces replace transpose with conjugate transpose in the standard inner product; the core examples here are real.'
],formula:'uᵀv = Σᵢuᵢvᵢ   ·   ||u||₂ = √(uᵀu)   ·   cos θ = uᵀv/(||u||₂||v||₂)',steps:['Take u=(3,4),v=(4,−3).','uᵀv=12−12=0, so the vectors are perpendicular.','Both norms are 5. Their normalized versions are (0.6,0.8) and (0.8,−0.6).','For w=(1,1), ||w||₁=2, ||w||₂=√2 and ||w||∞=1.'],application:'Cosine similarity compares directions of document or embedding vectors; Euclidean distance compares points by ||x−y||₂.',trap:'Orthogonal means dot product zero. Orthonormal additionally requires unit length.',task:'Find the norm and unit vector for (−2,1,2).',answer:'Norm 3; unit vector (−2/3,1/3,2/3).',code:np+`u=np.array([3.,4.]);v=np.array([4.,-3.])
print('dot',u@v)
print('unit',u/np.linalg.norm(u))`,solution:np+`u=np.array([-2.,1.,2.])
print(np.linalg.norm(u))
print(u/np.linalg.norm(u))`,check:q('Vectors (1,1) and (1,−1) are…',['Orthogonal but not unit vectors','Orthonormal','Dependent','At an acute angle'],0,'Their dot product is zero and each norm is √2.'),visual:'vectors'});
ml(lm,{title:'Linear transformations and the column picture',p:[
'A linear map T preserves addition and scaling: T(a u+b v)=aT(u)+bT(v). Consequently T(0)=0. A map can preserve zero without being linear, so use both defining properties. Translation by a nonzero vector is affine rather than linear.',
'A matrix stores the images of basis vectors as its columns. For A with columns a₁,…,aₙ, Ax=x₁a₁+…+xₙaₙ. An m×n matrix maps Rⁿ into Rᵐ. The output dimension comes from the number of rows, not columns.',
'A 2D rotation, reflection, scaling or shear can be linear. A singular map flattens some nonzero direction to zero. Matrix multiplication corresponds to composing maps: AB applies B first and then A to a column vector. Reversing that order generally changes the result.',
'Use the images of (1,0) and (0,1) to predict an entire grid transformation. Straight lines remain straight or collapse to points; parallel lines remain parallel or can collapse. This geometric view makes determinants, eigenvectors and singular values easier to understand later.'
],formula:'A=[T(e₁) … T(eₙ)]   ⇒   Ax=ΣᵢxᵢT(eᵢ)   ·   (AB)x=A(Bx)',steps:['Let A=[[1,1],[0,1]]. Its columns are (1,0) and (1,1).','For x=(2,3), Ax=2(1,0)+3(1,1)=(5,3).','Horizontal coordinates shift by the vertical coordinate: this is a shear.','The origin is fixed and det(A)=1, so area is preserved.'],application:'Coordinate changes in graphics and linear feature transformations in data pipelines use the same column picture.',trap:'The rightmost matrix acts first when transformations act on column vectors.',task:'Apply a 90° counterclockwise rotation to (2,1).',answer:'Using [[0,−1],[1,0]] gives (−1,2).',code:np+`A=np.array([[1,1],[0,1]])
print(A@np.array([2,3]))`,solution:np+`R=np.array([[0,-1],[1,0]])
print(R@np.array([2,1]))`,check:q('Which map is not linear on R²?',['T(x)=x+(1,0)','T(x)=2x','T(x)=0','T(x₁,x₂)=(x₂,x₁)'],0,'A nonzero translation does not send zero to zero.'),visual:'transform'});
lm=mathModule('la','spaces','Spaces, subspaces & bases','Axioms, closure tests, independence, dimension and coordinates');
ml(lm,{title:'Vector spaces and the subspace test',p:[
'A vector space is a set with addition and scalar multiplication satisfying the vector-space axioms: associativity and commutativity of addition, a zero vector, additive inverses, the two distributive laws, associative scalar action and multiplication by scalar 1. Scalars come from a specified field, usually R in this guide.',
'Examples include Rⁿ, all m×n real matrices, polynomials of degree at most d, and real-valued functions on a fixed domain. A polynomial is a vector of coefficients in a chosen basis. The zero polynomial belongs to the space even though its degree is usually treated specially.',
'For a subset W of an existing vector space, check that W is nonempty and is closed under a u+b v for every u,v in W and scalars a,b. Equivalently check zero, addition and scalar multiplication. One counterexample disproves a subspace; checking a few successful examples cannot prove closure.',
'A homogeneous equation such as x+2y−z=0 defines a subspace because linear combinations preserve the zero right side. The equation x+2y−z=1 is an affine plane and fails the zero test. Intersections of subspaces are subspaces. Their union is a subspace only when one contains the other.'
],formula:'W≤V ⇔ W≠∅ and a u+b v∈W for every u,v∈W and a,b∈R',steps:['Let W={(x,y,z):x+2y−z=0}.','Solve for x: x=−2y+z.','Every vector is y(−2,1,0)+z(1,0,1).','W is a plane through zero, spanned by those two independent vectors.'],application:'The solutions of homogeneous constraints form feasible directions; null spaces are a central example.',trap:'“Degree exactly d” is not a polynomial vector space: zero is excluded and cancellation can lower degree.',task:'Show that {(x,y):x≥0} is not a subspace.',answer:'(1,0) belongs, but scalar multiplication by −1 gives (−1,0), which does not.',code:np+`B=np.array([[-2,1],[1,0],[0,1]])
x=B@np.array([3,4])
print(x)
print(np.array([1,2,-1])@x)`,solution:np+`v=np.array([1,0])
print('v allowed',bool(v[0]>=0))
print('-v allowed',bool((-v)[0]>=0))`,check:q('Which is a subspace of R²?',['x+y=0','x+y=1','x≥0','x²+y²=1'],0,'It is the solution set of a homogeneous linear equation.'),visual:'span'});
ml(lm,{title:'Dependence, independence and redundancy',p:[
'Vectors v₁,…,vₖ are independent when c₁v₁+…+cₖvₖ=0 has only the all-zero coefficient solution. Dependence means a nontrivial coefficient choice gives zero. In a dependent finite set, at least one vector can be written as a combination of the others.',
'Place the vectors as columns in a matrix A. Independence becomes the statement that Ax=0 has only x=0. Row reduction locates pivot columns; every column must be a pivot for column independence. More than n vectors in Rⁿ are always dependent.',
'Any set containing the zero vector is dependent. Two nonzero vectors are dependent exactly when they are scalar multiples. For three or more vectors, pairwise nonparallel directions do not guarantee independence: three vectors in a plane are necessarily dependent.',
'Removing a dependent vector does not shrink the span if it is a combination of the retained vectors. Adding a vector outside the span increases dimension by one. These observations explain why feature redundancy lowers matrix rank and why choosing a basis removes redundant descriptions.'
],formula:'[v₁ … vₖ]c=0 has only c=0 ⇔ {v₁,…,vₖ} is independent',steps:['Let v₁=(1,0),v₂=(0,1),v₃=(1,1).','v₁+v₂−v₃=0 is a nontrivial relation.','All three pairs are nonparallel, but the three-vector set is dependent.','Keep v₁,v₂ to preserve the same span R² without redundancy.'],application:'Duplicate or linearly redundant predictors make least-squares coefficients nonunique unless additional choices are imposed.',trap:'Independent rows and independent columns have different counts; for a rectangular matrix, say which independence you mean.',task:'Find a dependence relation for (1,2),(2,4).',answer:'2(1,2)−(2,4)=0, so the coefficients (2,−1) are nontrivial.',code:np+`A=np.array([[1,0,1],[0,1,1]])
print(A@np.array([1,1,-1]))
print('rank',np.linalg.matrix_rank(A))`,solution:np+`A=np.array([[1,2],[2,4]])
print(A@np.array([2,-1]))`,check:q('Three vectors in R² that are pairwise nonparallel are…',['Still linearly dependent','Necessarily independent','A basis of R³','All zero'],0,'A space of dimension 2 cannot contain 3 independent vectors.'),visual:'span'});
ml(lm,{title:'Basis, dimension and change of coordinates',p:[
'A basis is an independent spanning set. Every vector in the space has a unique coordinate representation in that basis: spanning gives existence, independence gives uniqueness. The number of basis vectors is the dimension; all bases of the same finite-dimensional space have the same size.',
'The standard basis of Rⁿ consists of coordinate unit vectors. P_d has basis 1,x,…,xᵈ and dimension d+1. The space of m×n matrices has dimension mn. A basis of a subspace of Rⁿ may have fewer than n vectors.',
'If B is a square matrix whose columns are a basis, actual coordinates and basis coordinates satisfy x=B[x]_B. Solve Bc=x to obtain c=[x]_B. For two bases B and C, [x]_C=C⁻¹B[x]_B. The vector itself has not changed, only its description.',
'For subspaces U,W of a finite-dimensional V, dim(U+W)=dim U+dim W−dim(U∩W). A direct sum has intersection {0}, giving unique decomposition and adding dimensions. Coordinate transformations and rank arguments use this counting relationship repeatedly.'
],formula:'x=B[x]_B   ·   [x]_C=C⁻¹B[x]_B   ·   dim(U+W)=dim U+dim W−dim(U∩W)',steps:['Choose B=[(1,1),(1,−1)] and x=(4,2).','The coordinate equations are c₁+c₂=4 and c₁−c₂=2.','Therefore c₁=3,c₂=1, so [x]_B=(3,1).','The standard coordinates (4,2) and B-coordinates (3,1) describe the same vector.'],application:'Eigenvector bases and singular-vector bases simplify a transformation by choosing useful coordinates.',trap:'An independent set need not span the whole ambient space; specify the space for which it is a basis.',task:'Find the coordinates of (2,4) in the same basis.',answer:'c₁+c₂=2 and c₁−c₂=4 give (3,−1).',code:np+`B=np.array([[1.,1.],[1.,-1.]])
print(np.linalg.solve(B,np.array([4.,2.])))`,solution:np+`B=np.array([[1.,1.],[1.,-1.]])
print(np.linalg.solve(B,np.array([2.,4.])))`,check:q('The real polynomials of degree at most 3 have dimension…',['4','3','2','Infinite'],0,'The coefficients of 1,x,x²,x³ are four independent parameters.'),visual:'transform'});
lm=mathModule('la','matrices','Matrix operations & blocks','Dimensions, products, transpose, inverse and partitioned matrices');
ml(lm,{title:'Matrix arithmetic without shape mistakes',p:[
'An m×n matrix has m rows and n columns. Addition requires the same shape and is componentwise. Scalar multiplication scales every entry. For A of shape m×n and B of shape n×p, AB has shape m×p; the inner dimensions must agree.',
'Each entry (AB)ᵢⱼ is a dot product of row i of A with column j of B. Equivalently, each output column is A times the corresponding column of B. Matrix multiplication is associative and distributive, but generally not commutative.',
'The identity I has ones on the diagonal and acts as a neutral transformation. The zero matrix sends every vector to zero. A product AB can be zero even when both factors are nonzero, because B’s outputs may lie in A’s null space.',
'In NumPy, @ means matrix multiplication; * means entrywise multiplication for arrays. A 1D array has no distinct row/column orientation, so use explicit reshaping when a calculation needs an n×1 column or 1×n row. Check shapes before checking arithmetic.'
],formula:'(AB)ᵢⱼ=ΣₖAᵢₖBₖⱼ   ·   (m×n)(n×p)→m×p',steps:['A=[[1,2],[0,1]], B=[[2,0],[1,3]].','AB first row is (1·2+2·1,1·0+2·3)=(4,6).','AB second row is (1,3), so AB=[[4,6],[1,3]].','BA=[[2,4],[1,5]], confirming that order matters.'],application:'A batch of observations multiplied by a weight matrix is the same operation as many weighted feature combinations.',trap:'AB=AC does not imply B=C unless cancellation by A is justified, for example A is invertible.',task:'Compute AB and BA for A=[[1,1],[0,1]], B=[[1,0],[1,1]].',answer:'AB=[[2,1],[1,1]], BA=[[1,1],[1,2]].',code:np+`A=np.array([[1,2],[0,1]]);B=np.array([[2,0],[1,3]])
print(A@B)
print(B@A)`,solution:np+`A=np.array([[1,1],[0,1]]);B=np.array([[1,0],[1,1]])
print(A@B);print(B@A)`,check:q('A is 3×2 and B is 2×4. AB is…',['3×4','2×2','4×3','Undefined'],0,'The inner dimensions 2 agree; the outer dimensions determine the result.'),visual:'transform'});
ml(lm,{title:'Transpose, inverse and useful identities',p:[
'The transpose exchanges rows and columns: (Aᵀ)ᵢⱼ=Aⱼᵢ. It reverses a product: (AB)ᵀ=BᵀAᵀ. The trace is the sum of diagonal entries of a square matrix. For compatible products, tr(AB)=tr(BA); this does not mean AB=BA.',
'A square matrix is invertible when there is a matrix A⁻¹ with AA⁻¹=A⁻¹A=I. Invertibility is equivalent to full rank, nonzero determinant, a zero-dimensional null space and a unique solution Ax=b for every b. A rectangular matrix has no two-sided inverse of this kind.',
'For invertible A and B, (AB)⁻¹=B⁻¹A⁻¹. Also (Aᵀ)⁻¹=(A⁻¹)ᵀ. There is no general identity (A+B)⁻¹=A⁻¹+B⁻¹. To solve Ax=b numerically, prefer a direct solve or factorization over explicitly forming the inverse.',
'For A=[[a,b],[c,d]], the inverse is [[d,−b],[−c,a]]/(ad−bc) when the denominator is nonzero. This formula is excellent for 2×2 exam arithmetic; it is not a recommendation to compute large inverses by cofactors.'
],formula:'(AB)ᵀ=BᵀAᵀ   ·   (AB)⁻¹=B⁻¹A⁻¹   ·   A⁻¹=(1/(ad−bc))[[d,−b],[−c,a]]',steps:['A=[[2,1],[1,1]] has determinant 1.','Its inverse is [[1,−1],[−1,2]].','For b=(3,2), x=A⁻¹b=(1,1).','Verify by multiplying A(1,1)=(3,2), rather than trusting the formula alone.'],application:'Changing coordinates and recovering an input from a reversible linear transform both use invertibility.',trap:'A matrix with a zero diagonal entry can still be invertible. [[0,1],[1,0]] is a counterexample.',task:'Invert [[1,2],[3,4]] and verify its product with the original.',answer:'The inverse is [[−2,1],[1.5,−0.5]]. Both products equal I.',code:np+`A=np.array([[2.,1.],[1.,1.]])
print(np.linalg.inv(A))
print(np.linalg.solve(A,np.array([3.,2.])))`,solution:np+`A=np.array([[1.,2.],[3.,4.]])
B=np.linalg.inv(A)
print(B);print(np.allclose(A@B,np.eye(2)))`,check:q('For invertible A,B, (AB)⁻¹ equals…',['B⁻¹A⁻¹','A⁻¹B⁻¹ always','A⁻¹+B⁻¹','AB'],0,'Undo A first, then undo B; matrix products apply rightmost factors first.'),visual:'transform'});
ml(lm,{title:'Partitioned matrices and Schur complements',p:[
'A partitioned or block matrix groups rows and columns into submatrices. The underlying matrix is unchanged. Block addition and multiplication use the same rules as scalar entries, but each block product must have compatible dimensions and its order must be preserved.',
'For [[A,B],[C,D]] times [[E,F],[G,H]], the upper-left block is AE+BG and the other blocks follow row-by-column multiplication. Block-diagonal matrices act independently on coordinate groups. Their determinant is the product of block determinants.',
'When A is invertible, eliminate C by block row operations. The remaining lower-right block is S=D−CA⁻¹B, called the Schur complement of A. The determinant identity det(M)=det(A)det(S) follows. It requires A invertible in this form; do not use A⁻¹ when A is singular.',
'If both A and S are invertible, solve block equations by substitution: y=S⁻¹(g−CA⁻¹f), then x=A⁻¹(f−By). A block inverse exists and can be built from these expressions. Directly replacing det([[A,B],[C,D]]) with det(AD−BC) is not generally valid for arbitrary noncommuting blocks.'
],formula:'S=D−CA⁻¹B   ·   det([[A,B],[C,D]])=det(A)det(S)  [A invertible]',steps:['For M=[[2,1],[1,3]], use scalar blocks A=2,B=C=1,D=3.','S=3−1·(1/2)·1=2.5.','det(M)=2·2.5=5, matching 2·3−1·1.','For block sizes larger than one, the multiplication order in CA⁻¹B becomes essential.'],application:'Block elimination appears when separating parameter groups in least squares and covariance calculations.',trap:'A partition is a grouping device; it does not make matrix blocks commute.',task:'Use a Schur complement to compute det([[4,2],[2,3]]).',answer:'S=3−2(1/4)2=2; determinant=4·2=8.',code:np+`A=np.array([[2.]]);B=np.array([[1.]]);C=B.T;D=np.array([[3.]])
S=D-C@np.linalg.solve(A,B)
print(S)
print(round(np.linalg.det(A)*np.linalg.det(S),4))`,solution:np+`A=np.array([[4.]]);B=np.array([[2.]]);D=np.array([[3.]])
S=D-B.T@np.linalg.solve(A,B)
print(round(float(np.linalg.det(A)*np.linalg.det(S)),4))`,check:q('The Schur complement of invertible A in [[A,B],[C,D]] is…',['D−CA⁻¹B','D−BA⁻¹C always','A−BD⁻¹C always','AD−BC in every case'],0,'Eliminating the lower-left block gives D−CA⁻¹B.'),visual:'blocks'});
lm=mathModule('la','special','Special matrices','Symmetry, orthogonality, idempotence and projection operators');
ml(lm,{title:'Symmetric, skew-symmetric and triangular matrices',p:[
'A real square matrix is symmetric if Aᵀ=A and skew-symmetric if Aᵀ=−A. A skew-symmetric real matrix has zero diagonal, because aᵢᵢ=−aᵢᵢ. Every real square matrix splits uniquely into a symmetric and a skew-symmetric part.',
'The decomposition is A=(A+Aᵀ)/2+(A−Aᵀ)/2. For a real vector x, xᵀKx=0 when K is skew-symmetric, so a quadratic expression xᵀAx depends only on the symmetric part of A. This fact prevents unnecessary quadratic-form calculations.',
'A diagonal matrix has no off-diagonal entries. Upper triangular means entries below the diagonal are zero; lower triangular is the reverse. Products of upper triangular matrices remain upper triangular. A triangular matrix’s determinant and eigenvalues come directly from its diagonal.',
'A real symmetric matrix has real eigenvalues and an orthonormal eigenbasis. Products of two symmetric matrices need not be symmetric: AB is symmetric exactly when AB=BA for symmetric A,B. Distinguish a condition on each factor from a condition on their product.'
],formula:'A=S+K,   S=(A+Aᵀ)/2,   K=(A−Aᵀ)/2   ·   xᵀKx=0',steps:['For A=[[1,4],[2,3]], S=[[1,3],[3,3]].','K=[[0,1],[−1,0]], and S+K=A.','For x=(2,1), xᵀKx=2−2=0.','Therefore xᵀAx=xᵀSx=19.'],application:'Covariance matrices are symmetric; optimization curvature uses a symmetric Hessian when second derivatives are continuous.',trap:'A zero diagonal does not imply skew-symmetry; the paired off-diagonal entries must also be negatives.',task:'Split [[2,5],[1,4]] into symmetric and skew-symmetric parts.',answer:'S=[[2,3],[3,4]], K=[[0,2],[−2,0]].',code:np+`A=np.array([[1.,4.],[2.,3.]])
S=(A+A.T)/2;K=(A-A.T)/2;x=np.array([2.,1.])
print(S);print(K);print(x@A@x,x@S@x)`,solution:np+`A=np.array([[2.,5.],[1.,4.]])
print((A+A.T)/2);print((A-A.T)/2)`,check:q('For a real skew-symmetric K and real x, xᵀKx is…',['0','Always positive','Always negative','The determinant of K'],0,'The scalar equals its transpose, which also equals its negative.'),visual:'quadratic'});
ml(lm,{title:'Orthogonal matrices preserve geometry',p:[
'A real square matrix Q is orthogonal when QᵀQ=I. Its columns form an orthonormal basis, and its rows do too. Q⁻¹=Qᵀ. A rectangular matrix may have orthonormal columns, but is not an orthogonal square matrix under this convention.',
'Orthogonal transformations preserve dot products: (Qx)ᵀ(Qy)=xᵀy. Consequently they preserve Euclidean lengths, distances and angles. Rotations and reflections are examples. Orthogonal matrices can have negative entries; “orthogonal” is not a statement about all entries being zero except the diagonal.',
'Taking determinants of QᵀQ=I gives det(Q)²=1, so det(Q)=±1. A positive determinant preserves orientation; a negative determinant reverses it. Real eigenvalues of an orthogonal matrix can only be ±1, while complex eigenvalues have magnitude one.',
'For a rectangular m×r matrix U with UᵀU=I_r, multiplication preserves lengths of vectors in Rʳ, but UUᵀ is a projection in Rᵐ rather than generally I_m. This distinction is crucial in thin QR and reduced SVD.'
],formula:'QᵀQ=I ⇒ Q⁻¹=Qᵀ,  ||Qx||₂=||x||₂,  det Q=±1',steps:['Q=[[0,−1],[1,0]] rotates by 90°.','QᵀQ=I and det Q=1.','For x=(3,4), Qx=(−4,3). Both vectors have norm 5.','A reflection diag(1,−1) also preserves length but has determinant −1.'],application:'Orthogonal coordinate changes avoid stretching numerical errors and are central to stable decompositions.',trap:'If U is tall with orthonormal columns, UᵀU=I does not imply UUᵀ=I.',task:'Verify that diag(1,−1) is orthogonal and reflects (2,3).',answer:'Its transpose times itself is I; the output is (2,−3), with the same norm.',code:np+`Q=np.array([[0.,-1.],[1.,0.]])
x=np.array([3.,4.])
print(Q.T@Q);print(Q@x);print(np.linalg.norm(Q@x))`,solution:np+`Q=np.diag([1.,-1.]);x=np.array([2.,3.])
print(Q.T@Q);print(Q@x)`,check:q('A real square orthogonal matrix has determinant…',['Either +1 or −1','Only +1','Only 0','Any positive number'],0,'det(QᵀQ)=det(Q)²=1.'),visual:'transform'});
ml(lm,{title:'Idempotent matrices and projection matrices',p:[
'An idempotent matrix satisfies P²=P: applying it twice has the same effect as applying it once. Its image consists of fixed vectors, and its kernel consists of vectors sent to zero. Every vector x decomposes as Px+(I−P)x into image and kernel components.',
'For an eigenpair Pv=λv, idempotence gives λ²=λ, hence λ is 0 or 1. Because the polynomial t(t−1) has distinct roots, an idempotent matrix is diagonalizable. Its trace equals its rank. I−P is also idempotent and projects onto the complementary component.',
'A real orthogonal projection onto a subspace is both symmetric and idempotent. An idempotent matrix that is not symmetric is an oblique projection: its residual need not be perpendicular to its image. Some questions use “projection matrix” broadly; state whether orthogonal projection is intended.',
'For a unit vector u, P=uuᵀ projects onto its line. For orthonormal columns U, P=UUᵀ projects onto their span. If the columns of A are independent but not orthonormal, P=A(AᵀA)⁻¹Aᵀ. A rank-deficient A requires a pseudoinverse or an independent basis instead.'
],formula:'P²=P; orthogonal projection also has Pᵀ=P.   P_line=uuᵀ/(uᵀu), u≠0',steps:['Project onto the line spanned by u=(1,1).','P=(1/2)[[1,1],[1,1]].','For x=(3,1), Px=(2,2), and residual (1,−1) is perpendicular to u.','P²=P, tr(P)=1 and rank(P)=1.'],application:'Least-squares fitted values are projections onto a column space; the residual is the part the model cannot represent.',trap:'Idempotence alone does not imply orthogonal projection. [[1,1],[0,0]] is idempotent but not symmetric.',task:'Build the projector onto (1,2) and project (3,0).',answer:'P=(1/5)[[1,2],[2,4]], giving (0.6,1.2).',code:np+`u=np.array([1.,1.]);P=np.outer(u,u)/(u@u)
print(P);print(P@np.array([3.,1.]));print(np.allclose(P@P,P))`,solution:np+`u=np.array([1.,2.]);P=np.outer(u,u)/(u@u)
print(P);print(P@np.array([3.,0.]))`,check:q('A real matrix defines an orthogonal projector exactly when it is…',['Symmetric and idempotent','Only invertible','Only symmetric','Only triangular'],0,'Idempotence gives a projection; symmetry makes its image and residual orthogonal.'),visual:'projection'});
lm=mathModule('la','systems','Systems & Gaussian elimination','Augmented matrices, pivots, consistency and parametric solutions');
ml(lm,{title:'Translate equations into Ax=b',p:[
'A system of m linear equations in n unknowns is Ax=b, where each row stores one equation’s coefficients. A solution makes all equations true simultaneously. Geometrically in R², two nonparallel lines intersect once, parallel distinct lines never meet, and coincident lines have infinitely many common points.',
'The column picture says b must belong to the column space of A. The augmented matrix [A|b] records both coefficients and the right side. Elementary row operations—swapping rows, scaling a row by a nonzero scalar and adding a multiple of another row—preserve the solution set.',
'A homogeneous system has b=0 and always admits x=0. It has nonzero solutions exactly when its columns are dependent, or equivalently rank(A)<n. For a nonhomogeneous consistent system, the general solution is x_p+z with z in the null space.',
'The number of equations alone does not determine the number of solutions. Dependent or contradictory equations can appear in square, tall or wide systems. Count pivots and test consistency instead of assuming “more equations means no solution”.'
],formula:'Ax=b is consistent ⇔ b∈Col(A) ⇔ rank(A)=rank([A|b])',steps:['Solve x+y=3 and x−y=1.','Adding equations gives 2x=4, so x=2.','Substitute to obtain y=1.','The matrix columns (1,1) and (1,−1) combine as 2(1,1)+(1,−1)=(3,1).'],application:'Resource balances, network flows and interpolation conditions become linear systems.',trap:'A homogeneous system is always consistent, even when A is singular.',task:'Compare x+y=2, 2x+2y=4 with x+y=2, 2x+2y=5.',answer:'The first has x=2−t,y=t for any real t. The second reduces to 0=1 and is inconsistent.',code:np+`A=np.array([[1.,1.],[1.,-1.]])
b=np.array([3.,1.]);x=np.linalg.solve(A,b)
print(x);print(A@x)`,solution:np+`A=np.array([[1.,1.],[2.,2.]])
for b in [np.array([2.,4.]),np.array([2.,5.])]:
    print(np.linalg.matrix_rank(A),np.linalg.matrix_rank(np.column_stack([A,b])))`,check:q('Ax=0 has a nonzero solution exactly when…',['rank(A)<number of columns','rank(A)=number of columns','A has more rows than columns','b is outside Col(A)'],0,'A free variable exists precisely when a column lacks a pivot.'),visual:'elimination'});
ml(lm,{title:'Gaussian elimination, echelon form and pivoting',p:[
'Gaussian elimination uses a pivot to remove entries below it, producing row echelon form. Leading nonzero entries move right as you move down rows; zero rows go to the bottom. Back substitution then determines pivot variables from the last nonzero row upward.',
'Reduced row echelon form (RREF) additionally makes each pivot equal to one and the only nonzero entry in its column. RREF is unique, although the sequence of row operations is not. Ordinary echelon form is not unique. Free columns identify variables that may be chosen independently in a consistent system.',
'If a pivot position is zero, search lower rows for a nonzero entry and swap; if none exists, skip that column. Numerical partial pivoting chooses a largest-magnitude available entry in the column to reduce instability from division by tiny values. In exact hand arithmetic, keep fractions rather than rounding early.',
'Dense elimination is O(n³) for a general square system; back substitution in an already triangular system is O(n²). Row operations preserve rank and solutions, but not determinant without tracking the operation’s effect. They also do not generally preserve eigenvalues.'
],formula:'Rᵢ ← Rᵢ − (aᵢₖ/aₖₖ)Rₖ   [nonzero pivot aₖₖ]',steps:['For x+2y=5 and 3x+4y=11, start with [[1,2|5],[3,4|11]].','R₂←R₂−3R₁ gives [[1,2|5],[0,−2|−4]].','Back substitution: y=2, then x=1.','The row multiplier 3 will become an entry in L in LU factorization.'],application:'One elimination factorization can solve many systems with the same coefficient matrix and different right sides.',trap:'A zero pivot at the current position does not prove singularity; a row exchange may repair it.',task:'Solve 2y=4 and x+y=3, explicitly swapping the initial zero-pivot row.',answer:'Swap equations first; y=2 then x=1.',code:np+`M=np.array([[1.,2.,5.],[3.,4.,11.]])
M[1]-=3*M[0]
print(M)
y=M[1,2]/M[1,1];x=M[0,2]-M[0,1]*y
print(x,y)`,solution:np+`M=np.array([[0.,2.,4.],[1.,1.,3.]])
M[[0,1]]=M[[1,0]]
y=M[1,2]/M[1,1];x=M[0,2]-M[0,1]*y
print(M);print(x,y)`,check:q('A zero first pivot in [[0,1],[1,0]] means…',['Swap rows; the system can still be uniquely solvable','The matrix must be singular','There must be infinitely many solutions','Elimination is impossible'],0,'A row swap exposes nonzero pivots; the determinant is −1.'),visual:'elimination'});
ml(lm,{title:'Free variables and parameter-dependent systems',p:[
'A row [0 … 0|c] with c≠0 is a contradiction. If no such row exists, the system is consistent. A consistent system with rank r and n unknowns has n−r free parameters. If r=n the solution is unique; otherwise infinitely many solutions exist over R.',
'Express pivot variables in terms of free variables to obtain x=x_p+t₁v₁+…+tₖvₖ. The vectors vᵢ form a basis of the homogeneous solution space. The nonhomogeneous solution set is an affine translate of that subspace, not generally itself a subspace.',
'When a matrix contains a parameter, do not divide by an expression that might be zero without separating cases. A pivot such as a−2 creates one generic case a≠2 and a special case a=2. Recheck the right side in the special case to distinguish inconsistency from infinitely many solutions.',
'For square systems, a nonzero determinant guarantees uniqueness. A zero determinant only says uniqueness fails; it does not decide consistency. Use the augmented system at each exceptional parameter value.'
],formula:'Consistent: x=x_p+N t, where columns of N span Null(A); number of parameters=n−rank(A)',steps:['Consider x+y=2 and x+a y=3. Subtract the first equation.','(a−1)y=1. If a≠1, y=1/(a−1), x=2−y.','If a=1, the equations imply 0=1, so no solution.','If the second right side were 2 instead, a=1 would produce infinitely many solutions.'],application:'Identifiability asks whether model parameters are uniquely determined or can vary along null-space directions.',trap:'“Determinant zero” does not distinguish zero solutions from infinitely many solutions.',task:'Solve x+2y+z=3 and y−z=1 in parametric form.',answer:'Set z=t. Then y=1+t and x=1−3t, so x-vector=(1,1,0)+t(−3,1,1).',code:np+`for a in [1.,2.,3.]:
    A=np.array([[1.,1.],[1.,a]]);b=np.array([2.,3.])
    print(a,np.linalg.matrix_rank(A),np.linalg.matrix_rank(np.column_stack([A,b])))`,solution:np+`A=np.array([[1.,2.,1.],[0.,1.,-1.]])
p=np.array([1.,1.,0.]);v=np.array([-3.,1.,1.])
print(A@p);print(A@v)`,check:q('A consistent system with 5 unknowns and rank 3 has…',['2 free parameters','3 free parameters','5 free parameters','A unique solution'],0,'Nullity is 5−3=2.'),visual:'elimination'});
lm=mathModule('la','rank','Rank, nullity & fundamental spaces','Pivot counting, kernels, column spaces and dimension identities');
ml(lm,{title:'Rank–nullity and the four fundamental spaces',p:[
'The column space of an m×n matrix is a subspace of Rᵐ; the row space is a subspace of Rⁿ. Both have dimension rank(A)=r. The null space consists of x in Rⁿ with Ax=0. The left null space consists of y in Rᵐ with Aᵀy=0.',
'Rank–nullity says dim Null(A)=n−r. Applying it to Aᵀ gives dim Null(Aᵀ)=m−r. Domain dimension is split into independent input directions that survive and directions collapsed to zero. Rank cannot exceed min(m,n).',
'Row reduction produces a row-space basis from the nonzero echelon rows. To obtain a column-space basis, identify pivot columns in the reduced matrix but select those columns from the original matrix. Row operations change the actual column vectors, even though they preserve their dependence relationships.',
'Rows are perpendicular to every null-space vector, so Row(A)=Null(A)⊥. Likewise Col(A)=Null(Aᵀ)⊥. The ambient spaces matter: a row-space vector has n entries while a column-space vector has m entries. Do not confuse their equal dimensions with equal sets.'
],formula:'A∈Rᵐˣⁿ, rank A=r ⇒ nullity A=n−r, nullity Aᵀ=m−r',steps:['A=[[1,2,3],[2,4,6]] has rank 1.','Its nullity is 3−1=2 and left nullity is 2−1=1.','A column-space basis is {(1,2)}. A row-space basis is {(1,2,3)}.','Null(A) has basis {(-2,1,0),(-3,0,1)}.'],application:'Rank measures the number of independent features or independent output directions in a data matrix.',trap:'Use pivot columns of the original matrix for a column-space basis, not the altered columns of RREF.',task:'For a 4×7 matrix of rank 3, find nullity and left nullity.',answer:'Nullity 4; left nullity 1.',code:np+`A=np.array([[1.,2.,3.],[2.,4.,6.]])
r=np.linalg.matrix_rank(A)
print('rank',r,'nullity',A.shape[1]-r,'left nullity',A.shape[0]-r)`,solution:`m,n,r=4,7,3
print(n-r,m-r)`,check:q('A 3×5 matrix of rank 2 has nullity…',['3','1','2','5'],0,'Nullity uses the number of columns: 5−2.'),visual:'rank'});
ml(lm,{title:'Rank inequalities and invertible transformations',p:[
'Multiplication by an invertible matrix on either side preserves rank. Row operations are multiplication by invertible elementary matrices, explaining rank preservation. Transposition also preserves rank. Multiplication by a singular matrix can reduce rank but cannot increase it beyond the factors’ ranks.',
'For compatible A and B, rank(AB)≤min(rank A,rank B). If A is m×n and B is n×p, Sylvester’s lower bound is rank(AB)≥rank A+rank B−n. Also rank(A+B)≤rank A+rank B for same-shaped matrices.',
'For real A, Null(AᵀA)=Null(A), since xᵀAᵀAx=||Ax||². Therefore rank(AᵀA)=rank(A). Full column rank is equivalent to AᵀA being positive definite and invertible. This argument relies on the conjugate transpose in the complex case.',
'Rank is exact in algebra but tolerance-dependent in floating-point software. A tiny singular value may be treated as numerically zero. Use exact symbolic reasoning for exam matrices; in data analysis inspect singular values, scaling and the tolerance rather than treating a reported rank as an absolute physical fact.'
],formula:'rank A+rank B−n ≤ rank(AB) ≤ min(rank A,rank B)   ·   rank(AᵀA)=rank A',steps:['A is 4×5 with rank 4; B is 5×3 with rank 3.','The product is 4×3. Its rank is at most 3.','The lower bound is 4+3−5=2.','So rank(AB) can only be 2 or 3; factor ranks alone do not choose between them.'],application:'Rank bounds can prove information loss without multiplying a large pair of matrices.',trap:'rank(AB)=rank(A)rank(B) is not a valid identity.',task:'For 3×3 matrices A,B each of rank 2, give possible rank bounds on AB.',answer:'1≤rank(AB)≤2.',code:np+`A=np.array([[1.,2.,3.],[2.,4.,6.]])
print(np.linalg.matrix_rank(A),np.linalg.matrix_rank(A.T@A))`,solution:`rA,rB,n=2,2,3
print(max(0,rA+rB-n),min(rA,rB))`,check:q('For real A, rank(AᵀA) equals…',['rank(A)','2 rank(A)','rank(A)²','Always the number of columns'],0,'The two matrices have the same null space in the domain.'),visual:'rank'});
ml(lm,{title:'Orthogonal complements and solvability certificates',p:[
'The orthogonal complement W⊥ contains vectors perpendicular to every vector of W. In Rⁿ, dim W+dim W⊥=n and W∩W⊥={0}. Every vector has a unique orthogonal decomposition into a component in W and one in W⊥.',
'Ax=b is solvable exactly when b is orthogonal to every vector in Null(Aᵀ). Necessity follows from yᵀb=yᵀAx=0. Sufficiency follows because Col(A)=Null(Aᵀ)⊥. This is an alternative to directly row-reducing the augmented matrix.',
'A single y with Aᵀy=0 but yᵀb≠0 certifies inconsistency. It represents a linear combination of left-side equations cancelling to zero while the same combination of right sides does not. It is a compact proof of contradiction.',
'For least squares, the residual r=b−A x̂ belongs to Null(Aᵀ), while the fitted vector belongs to Col(A). This perpendicular decomposition is the geometric reason the normal equations work. No calculus is required to understand the decomposition itself.'
],formula:'Ax=b solvable ⇔ yᵀb=0 for every y with Aᵀy=0',steps:['A=[[1,1],[2,2]] and y=(−2,1). Then Aᵀy=0.','For b=(1,2), yᵀb=0, and the system is consistent.','For b=(1,3), yᵀb=1, proving inconsistency.','The certificate is exactly “second equation minus twice the first gives 0=1”.'],application:'Constraint infeasibility can be explained by a short certificate instead of a large list of failed numerical attempts.',trap:'Orthogonality to one left-null vector suffices only if it spans the entire left null space; otherwise test a basis.',task:'For A=[[1],[1],[1]], characterize all b for which Ax=b is solvable.',answer:'All three entries of b must be equal; b must lie on the line spanned by (1,1,1).',code:np+`A=np.array([[1.,1.],[2.,2.]]);y=np.array([-2.,1.])
print(A.T@y)
for b in [np.array([1.,2.]),np.array([1.,3.])]:print(y@b)`,solution:np+`A=np.ones((3,1))
for b in [np.array([2.,2.,2.]),np.array([2.,2.,3.])]:
    print(np.linalg.matrix_rank(A)==np.linalg.matrix_rank(np.column_stack([A,b])))`,check:q('If Aᵀy=0 but yᵀb≠0, then Ax=b…',['Has no solution','Has a unique solution','Has infinitely many solutions','Must have b=0'],0,'Multiplying a hypothetical solution by yᵀ would give a contradiction.'),visual:'projection'});
lm=mathModule('la','det','Determinants & invertibility','Area, row-operation rules, cofactors and parameter cases');
ml(lm,{title:'Determinants as signed volume scaling',p:[
'The determinant of a square matrix is its signed volume scale factor. In 2D, |det A| is the area of the parallelogram formed by its columns. A negative determinant reverses orientation. A zero determinant means the columns collapse volume into a lower-dimensional set.',
'For [[a,b],[c,d]], det A=ad−bc. In higher dimensions, the determinant is alternating and multilinear in the columns (or rows), normalized so det I=1. Swapping two columns changes its sign; equal or dependent columns make it zero.',
'A triangular matrix has determinant equal to the product of its diagonal entries. A square matrix is invertible exactly when its determinant is nonzero. A rectangular matrix does not have an ordinary determinant, even though its columns can span a volume through a Gram determinant.',
'Determinant is not a measure of all conditioning effects. A matrix can have determinant 1 yet stretch one direction by 10⁶ and compress another by 10⁻⁶. Singular values describe the directional effects more fully.'
],formula:'det [[a,b],[c,d]]=ad−bc   ·   area scale=|det A|   ·   det A≠0 ⇔ A invertible',steps:['A=[[2,1],[0,3]] sends the unit square to a parallelogram.','Its area is |2·3−0·1|=6.','Multiplying by a reflection changes the determinant sign but not the area magnitude.','If the second column were twice the first, the parallelogram would collapse and the determinant would be zero.'],application:'The determinant measures local area/volume scaling in coordinate transformations and detects loss of invertibility.',trap:'det(A+B) is not generally det(A)+det(B).',task:'Find determinant and orientation effect of [[1,3],[2,1]].',answer:'1−6=−5: area scales by 5 and orientation reverses.',code:np+`A=np.array([[2.,1.],[0.,3.]])
print(round(np.linalg.det(A),4))`,solution:np+`A=np.array([[1.,3.],[2.,1.]])
print(round(np.linalg.det(A),4))`,check:q('A 2×2 matrix with determinant −3 changes area by factor…',['3','−3','1/3','0'],0,'Area is nonnegative; the negative sign records orientation reversal.'),visual:'transform'});
ml(lm,{title:'Efficient determinant computation',p:[
'A row swap multiplies the determinant by −1. Multiplying one row by c multiplies it by c. Adding a multiple of one row to another leaves the determinant unchanged. Track these changes while reducing to triangular form, then multiply the diagonal.',
'Cofactor expansion along row i is Σⱼ aᵢⱼ(−1)ⁱ⁺ʲ det(Mᵢⱼ). Expand along a row or column with many zeros when doing small exact calculations. The adjugate satisfies A adj(A)=det(A)I, yielding A⁻¹=adj(A)/det(A) when invertible.',
'Useful identities are det(AB)=det A det B, det(Aᵀ)=det A and det(cA)=cⁿdet A for n×n A. If A is invertible, det(A⁻¹)=1/det A. Scaling the whole matrix scales every row, not just one row.',
'Cramer’s rule gives xᵢ=det(Aᵢ)/det A for a nonsingular square system, replacing column i by b. It is useful for proofs and small symbolic questions, but elimination is usually more efficient for large numerical systems.'
],formula:'Row add: unchanged; row swap: ×(−1); one-row scale c: ×c; det(cA)=cⁿdet A',steps:['A=[[1,2,0],[2,5,1],[0,1,3]].','R₂←R₂−2R₁ gives second row [0,1,1].','R₃←R₃−R₂ gives [0,0,2].','No swaps or row scalings occurred, so det A=1·1·2=2.'],application:'Elimination computes determinants and solves systems using essentially the same arithmetic.',trap:'For n×n A, multiplying every entry by 2 multiplies the determinant by 2ⁿ, not merely 2.',task:'If a 3×3 A has determinant 5, find det(2Aᵀ).',answer:'2³ det(Aᵀ)=8·5=40.',code:np+`A=np.array([[1.,2.,0.],[2.,5.,1.],[0.,1.,3.]])
print(round(np.linalg.det(A),4))`,solution:`n=3;detA=5
print(2**n*detA)`,check:q('Adding 7 times row 1 to row 2 changes determinant by…',['No change','A factor of 7','A sign reversal','Adding 7'],0,'An elementary row replacement preserves determinant.'),visual:'elimination'});
ml(lm,{title:'Determinant parameters and structural shortcuts',p:[
'For parameter-dependent matrices, compute a determinant polynomial and factor it to locate singular cases. Outside those roots the matrix is invertible. At a root, return to rank or the augmented system to decide the remaining properties.',
'Block-triangular matrices have determinant equal to the product of diagonal-block determinants, provided those blocks are square. For a matrix with a known invariant direction, an eigenvalue factor can simplify the determinant. A repeated row relationship can make a determinant zero without expansion.',
'If u and v are column vectors, the matrix determinant lemma gives det(A+uvᵀ)=det(A)(1+vᵀA⁻¹u) when A is invertible. In particular det(I+uvᵀ)=1+vᵀu. This is supporting advanced depth, useful when a matrix is an identity plus a rank-one update.',
'Always check the conditions of a shortcut. A singular A cannot be substituted into a formula requiring A⁻¹. If an expression includes a denominator in the parameter, preserve and inspect excluded parameter values instead of cancelling them silently.'
],formula:'det(I+uvᵀ)=1+vᵀu   ·   det [[A,B],[0,D]]=det A·det D',steps:['A(t)=[[t,1],[1,t]] has determinant t²−1=(t−1)(t+1).','It is singular exactly at t=1 or t=−1.','At t=1, both rows are identical and rank is 1.','For u=(1,2),v=(3,4), det(I+uvᵀ)=1+3+8=12.'],application:'Rank-one updates occur when adding a single observation or updating a covariance-like matrix.',trap:'A determinant root identifies singularity, not the entire solution behavior of Ax=b.',task:'Determine the singular parameter values of [[a,2],[2,a]].',answer:'a²−4=0, so a=±2.',code:np+`u=np.array([1.,2.]);v=np.array([3.,4.])
A=np.eye(2)+np.outer(u,v)
print(A);print(round(np.linalg.det(A),4),1+v@u)`,solution:np+`for a in [-2.,0.,2.,3.]:
    A=np.array([[a,2.],[2.,a]])
    print(a,round(np.linalg.det(A),4))`,check:q('det(I+uvᵀ), for real column vectors u,v, is…',['1+vᵀu','1+||u||||v|| always','det(u)det(v)','Always 1'],0,'This is the rank-one determinant identity; u and v themselves are not square matrices.'),visual:'transform'});
lm=mathModule('la','ortho','Orthogonality & least squares','Projections, Gram–Schmidt, QR and the best-fit interpretation');
ml(lm,{title:'Project a vector onto a line or subspace',p:[
'The orthogonal projection of b onto a nonzero vector a is p=a(aᵀb)/(aᵀa). The scalar coefficient is chosen so that residual b−p is perpendicular to a. The projection p lies on the line, and the residual measures the shortest displacement from b to that line.',
'For an orthonormal basis q₁,…,qᵣ of a subspace, add the line projections: p=Σqᵢ(qᵢᵀb). With Q holding those columns, p=QQᵀb. For an arbitrary independent basis A, solve AᵀA c=Aᵀb and set p=Ac.',
'The Pythagorean identity ||b||²=||p||²+||b−p||² holds because the components are perpendicular. For any other point p+w in the subspace, its squared error is ||b−p||²+||w||², proving the closest point is unique.',
'The projection vector is unique even if the spanning columns are redundant. The coefficient representation may then be nonunique. Use an independent basis, QR/SVD or the pseudoinverse; do not invert a singular Gram matrix.'
],formula:'p=a(aᵀb)/(aᵀa)   ·   P=A(AᵀA)⁻¹Aᵀ when A has independent columns',steps:['Let a=(1,2), b=(3,1).','aᵀb=5 and aᵀa=5, so p=(1,2).','The residual is (2,−1), with aᵀr=2−2=0.','The squared lengths are 10=5+5.'],application:'Denoising by subspace projection retains representable structure and removes the orthogonal residual.',trap:'The projection coefficient is aᵀb only when a is already a unit vector.',task:'Project (2,0) onto span(1,1), and find the squared error.',answer:'Projection (1,1), residual (1,−1), squared error 2.',code:np+`a=np.array([1.,2.]);b=np.array([3.,1.])
p=a*(a@b)/(a@a);r=b-p
print(p,r,a@r)`,solution:np+`a=np.array([1.,1.]);b=np.array([2.,0.])
p=a*(a@b)/(a@a);r=b-p
print(p,r@r)`,check:q('The residual of an orthogonal projection onto Col(A) satisfies…',['Aᵀr=0','Ar=0 always','r=0 always','r equals every column'],0,'The residual is perpendicular to every column.'),visual:'projection'});
ml(lm,{title:'Gram–Schmidt and QR factorization',p:[
'Gram–Schmidt turns independent vectors into an orthonormal basis for the same span. Normalize the first vector. For the next vector, subtract its projection on every earlier orthonormal direction, then normalize what remains. A zero remainder signals dependence.',
'With independent columns a₁,…,aₙ, define rᵢⱼ=qᵢᵀaⱼ for i<j, subtract Σᵢ<ⱼrᵢⱼqᵢ, and let rⱼⱼ be the norm of the remainder. These coefficients form an upper-triangular matrix R and A=QR, where Q has orthonormal columns.',
'Modified Gram–Schmidt and Householder QR improve numerical stability relative to classical Gram–Schmidt. This guide uses the exact geometry for hand work and NumPy’s QR for computation. Signs of columns and corresponding rows can vary between valid QR factorizations.',
'For full-column-rank least squares, solve Rx̂=Qᵀb. This avoids explicitly forming AᵀA, which can worsen conditioning. QR is supporting depth connecting the named projections and systems topics; LU and SVD are explicitly named in the official syllabus.'
],formula:'vⱼ=aⱼ−Σᵢ<ⱼ(qᵢᵀaⱼ)qᵢ; qⱼ=vⱼ/||vⱼ||; A=QR',steps:['a₁=(1,1), a₂=(1,0). Then q₁=(1,1)/√2.','q₁ᵀa₂=1/√2; subtract (1/2,1/2) from a₂.','The remainder is (1/2,−1/2); normalize to q₂=(1,−1)/√2.','R=[[√2,1/√2],[0,1/√2]] for this sign convention.'],application:'QR supplies a stable least-squares solver and orthonormal coordinates for a column space.',trap:'Subtract projections on the orthonormal vectors, not blindly on the original unnormalized columns.',task:'Orthogonalize (1,0,1) and (1,1,0); verify the remainder is orthogonal to the first.',answer:'Projection coefficient 1/2; remainder (1/2,1,−1/2), whose dot product with (1,0,1) is zero.',code:np+`A=np.array([[1.,1.],[1.,0.]])
Q,R=np.linalg.qr(A)
print(np.allclose(Q.T@Q,np.eye(2)))
print(np.allclose(Q@R,A))`,solution:np+`a=np.array([1.,0.,1.]);b=np.array([1.,1.,0.])
v=b-a*(a@b)/(a@a)
print(v,a@v)`,check:q('A zero Gram–Schmidt remainder indicates…',['The new vector lies in the previous span','The vectors are all unit length','The matrix must be square','The new vector is independent'],0,'Subtracting the entire span component leaves no new direction.'),visual:'projection'});
ml(lm,{title:'Least squares from geometry to regression',p:[
'When Ax=b is inconsistent, least squares chooses x̂ minimizing ||Ax−b||₂². The fitted vector Ax̂ is the orthogonal projection of b onto Col(A). Its residual is perpendicular to every column, yielding AᵀA x̂=Aᵀb, the normal equations.',
'If A has full column rank, AᵀA is invertible and x̂ is unique. If rank is deficient, fitted values are still unique but coefficients can differ by a null-space vector. The pseudoinverse selects the minimum-norm coefficient vector among least-squares solutions.',
'For a line y≈β₀+β₁t, put a column of ones and a column of t values into A. The intercept is then an ordinary coefficient. If the model omits the intercept, the fitted line is forced through the origin; this changes both the meaning and the solution.',
'Calculus gives the same result by differentiating the quadratic objective: gradient 2Aᵀ(Ax−b)=0. The projection derivation does not require calculus; use the linked gradient bridge when you want to connect the two viewpoints. Numerically prefer QR/SVD-based solvers over explicit normal-equation inversion.'
],formula:'minₓ ||Ax−b||² ⇒ Aᵀ(Ax̂−b)=0; full column rank ⇒ x̂=(AᵀA)⁻¹Aᵀb',steps:['Fit a constant c to b=(1,2,6); A is a column of three ones.','Normal equation: 3c=1+2+6=9, giving c=3.','Fitted values are (3,3,3), residual (−2,−1,3).','The residual sums to zero, so it is orthogonal to the column of ones.'],application:'Linear regression, calibration and fitting a trend to noisy measurements are least-squares problems.',trap:'An inconsistent system can have a unique least-squares minimizer even though it has no exact solution.',task:'Fit a line with intercept through noisy observations t=[0,1,2], y=[1,2,2].',answer:'β₀=7/6 and β₁=1/2; fitted values are 7/6,5/3,13/6.',code:np+`A=np.ones((3,1));b=np.array([1.,2.,6.])
x=np.linalg.lstsq(A,b,rcond=None)[0]
print(x);print(b-A@x);print(A.T@(b-A@x))`,solution:np+`t=np.array([0.,1.,2.]);A=np.column_stack([np.ones(3),t]);y=np.array([1.,2.,2.])
beta=np.linalg.lstsq(A,y,rcond=None)[0]
print(beta);print(A@beta)`,check:q('Full column rank guarantees which least-squares property?',['Unique coefficient minimizer','Zero residual for every b','A square A','A symmetric A'],0,'AᵀA is invertible, but b need not belong to the column space.'),visual:'least-squares',recap:'calc-bridge-2'});
lm=mathModule('la','eigen','Eigenvalues & eigenvectors','Invariant directions, multiplicities, diagonalization and spectral reasoning');
ml(lm,{title:'Find eigenvalues and eigenspaces',p:[
'An eigenvector v≠0 satisfies Av=λv: its direction is unchanged apart from stretching or reversal. The zero vector is never an eigenvector. Eigenvalues are defined for square matrices. An eigenvalue may be zero, corresponding to a nonzero vector in the null space.',
'Solve det(A−λI)=0 to find candidate eigenvalues, then solve (A−λI)v=0 for each eigenspace. Using det(λI−A) changes the polynomial by a sign in odd dimensions but leaves its roots unchanged. An eigenspace includes zero, even though eigenvectors exclude zero.',
'The trace equals the sum of eigenvalues and determinant their product, counting algebraic multiplicities over the complex numbers. A triangular matrix’s eigenvalues are its diagonal entries. A real matrix can have complex eigenvalues, for example a 90° planar rotation.',
'Eigenvectors for distinct eigenvalues are independent. Their lengths are arbitrary; any nonzero scalar multiple remains an eigenvector. A numerical library may choose different signs or normalizations, so verify Av=λv rather than comparing one expected printed vector exactly.'
],formula:'Av=λv, v≠0   ⇔   det(A−λI)=0 and v∈Null(A−λI)\{0}',steps:['A=[[2,1],[1,2]]. det(A−λI)=(2−λ)²−1=(λ−1)(λ−3).','For λ=3, v=(1,1); for λ=1, v=(1,−1).','The sum 3+1=4 equals trace; product 3 equals determinant.','Normalize these vectors if an orthonormal eigenbasis is needed.'],application:'Eigenvectors identify persistent modes of a repeated linear transformation and principal axes of symmetric matrices.',trap:'A repeated eigenvalue does not determine how many independent eigenvectors exist.',task:'Find eigenvalues of [[4,1],[0,2]] and one vector for each.',answer:'λ=4 has (1,0); λ=2 has (1,−2).',code:np+`A=np.array([[2.,1.],[1.,2.]])
w,V=np.linalg.eigh(A)
print(w)
print(np.allclose(A@V,V@np.diag(w)))`,solution:np+`A=np.array([[4.,1.],[0.,2.]])
for lam,v in [(4.,np.array([1.,0.])),(2.,np.array([1.,-2.]))]:print(np.allclose(A@v,lam*v))`,check:q('An eigenvector must be…',['Nonzero','Unit length always','Unique for its eigenvalue','A row of A'],0,'Any nonzero scalar multiple is also an eigenvector.'),visual:'eigen'});
ml(lm,{title:'Multiplicity and diagonalization',p:[
'Algebraic multiplicity counts how often an eigenvalue occurs as a characteristic-polynomial root. Geometric multiplicity is the dimension of its eigenspace, equal to nullity(A−λI). For each eigenvalue, geometric multiplicity lies between 1 and its algebraic multiplicity.',
'A square n×n matrix is diagonalizable over a field when it has n independent eigenvectors over that field. Put them in S; then A=SΛS⁻¹. Distinct eigenvalues guarantee diagonalizability, but are not necessary: I has a repeated eigenvalue and an entire space of eigenvectors.',
'The matrix [[1,1],[0,1]] has eigenvalue 1 twice but only one independent eigenvector, so it is not diagonalizable. Repeated roots require an eigenspace calculation. A real rotation may be diagonalizable over C while not over R, so state the field.',
'For a real symmetric A, the spectral theorem supplies an orthogonal diagonalization A=QΛQᵀ. Its eigenvectors for distinct eigenvalues are orthogonal, and within a repeated eigenspace one can choose an orthonormal basis. General nonsymmetric matrices do not guarantee orthogonality.'
],formula:'A=SΛS⁻¹ ⇔ n independent eigenvectors; symmetric real A ⇒ A=QΛQᵀ',steps:['For J=[[1,1],[0,1]], J−I=[[0,1],[0,0]].','The null equation forces v₂=0, leaving only span(1,0).','Algebraic multiplicity is 2 but geometric multiplicity is 1.','J is not diagonalizable, unlike I₂, whose eigenspace has dimension 2.'],application:'Diagonal coordinates decouple a linear system into independent scalar modes, making powers and stability analysis simpler.',trap:'A repeated eigenvalue neither guarantees nor rules out diagonalizability.',task:'Compare eigenspace dimensions for I₃ and [[2,1],[0,2]].',answer:'I₃ has eigenvalue 1 with eigenspace dimension 3. The 2×2 Jordan block has eigenvalue 2 with eigenspace dimension 1.',code:np+`J=np.array([[1.,1.],[0.,1.]])
print('eigenspace dimension',2-np.linalg.matrix_rank(J-np.eye(2)))
print('identity eigenspace dimension',2-np.linalg.matrix_rank(np.eye(2)-np.eye(2)))`,solution:np+`J=np.array([[2.,1.],[0.,2.]])
print(3-np.linalg.matrix_rank(np.eye(3)-np.eye(3)))
print(2-np.linalg.matrix_rank(J-2*np.eye(2)))`,check:q('Every real symmetric matrix is…',['Orthogonally diagonalizable','Invertible','Positive definite','A projection'],0,'The spectral theorem does not require positive eigenvalues or nonzero eigenvalues.'),visual:'eigen'});
ml(lm,{title:'Powers, similarity and eigenvalue shortcuts',p:[
'If Av=λv, then Aᵏv=λᵏv for nonnegative integers k. More generally p(A)v=p(λ)v for a polynomial p. If A is invertible, A⁻¹v=(1/λ)v. These facts transform eigenvalues without recalculating a full determinant.',
'Similar matrices B=S⁻¹AS represent the same linear map in different bases. They share characteristic polynomial, eigenvalues with multiplicities, determinant, trace and rank. Similarity is not the same as a row operation or a congruence QᵀAQ.',
'Cayley–Hamilton says a matrix satisfies its characteristic polynomial. For a 2×2 matrix, A²−tr(A)A+det(A)I=0. This reduces high powers to lower powers and can express an inverse when the determinant is nonzero.',
'For a diagonalizable matrix, Aᵏ=SΛᵏS⁻¹. Modes with |λ|<1 decay under repeated application; |λ|>1 modes grow. At |λ|=1, a defective matrix may still have polynomial growth, so eigenvalue magnitudes alone need care on the boundary. This dynamical viewpoint is advanced supporting depth.'
],formula:'p(A)v=p(λ)v   ·   A²−tr(A)A+det(A)I=0 for 2×2 A',steps:['For A=[[2,1],[1,2]], trace=4 and determinant=3.','Cayley–Hamilton gives A²=4A−3I.','Multiplying once more gives A³=4A²−3A=13A−12I.','The eigenvalues of A³ are 3³=27 and 1³=1.'],application:'Repeated transitions, iterative algorithms and linear dynamical systems can be analyzed mode by mode.',trap:'Row reduction preserves rank but does not generally preserve eigenvalues; similarity does.',task:'If eigenvalues of invertible A are 2 and −1, find those of A²+3I and A⁻¹.',answer:'A²+3I: 7 and 4. A⁻¹: 1/2 and −1.',code:np+`A=np.array([[2.,1.],[1.,2.]])
print(np.allclose(A@A,4*A-3*np.eye(2)))
print(np.linalg.matrix_power(A,3))`,solution:`eigenvalues=[2.,-1.]
print([x*x+3 for x in eigenvalues])
print([1/x for x in eigenvalues])`,check:q('If Av=λv, the eigenvalue of A²+2A+I on v is…',['(λ+1)²','λ²+1 only','λ+3','Always 1'],0,'Apply the polynomial to λ.'),visual:'eigen'});
